WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.496
Hey everyone, welcome back to
 mCoding with James&nbsp;Murphy. That's me!

00:00:03.496 --> 00:00:06.436
Today, I'm getting you up to 
speed with your ABCs.

00:00:06.436 --> 00:00:09.362
Wait, can I say that, or&nbsp;is this 
going to get flagged as "for kids"?

00:00:09.362 --> 00:00:14.322
No, not the alphabet ABCs of course, 
I'm talking&nbsp;about Abstract Base Classes.

00:00:14.322 --> 00:00:18.455
Specifically, the abstract collections 
defined&nbsp;in the Collections abc module.

00:00:18.455 --> 00:00:20.001
I'll talk about when and 
why you should&nbsp;use them,

00:00:20.001 --> 00:00:24.065
including both their typing and 
runtime use cases, and their mix-in&nbsp;use cases.

00:00:24.065 --> 00:00:25.573
Then, we'll finish up 
with an example

00:00:25.573 --> 00:00:29.565
where we use an abstract collection to&nbsp;
implement this "Invertible Dictionary" class.

00:00:29.565 --> 00:00:32.061
It's just like a dictionary that&nbsp;
you can use to map keys to values.

00:00:32.061 --> 00:00:35.204
But you can also map those values&nbsp;
back to their starting keys.

00:00:35.204 --> 00:00:38.606
Let's get started by taking a 
quick look at the&nbsp;Collections ABC docs.

00:00:38.606 --> 00:00:43.089
The first thing to notice is that 
there are quite a few of these ABCs.

00:00:43.089 --> 00:00:47.266
Their primary purpose is to serve as 
sort of an interface to some of the built-in tools.

00:00:47.266 --> 00:00:49.484
So, if you wanted to write 
your own version of that tool,

00:00:49.484 --> 00:00:53.820
you just need to implement&nbsp;
these methods to conform to that interface.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.379
Some are very simple interfaces, like the&nbsp;
`Iterable`, which just needs to define a&nbsp;&nbsp; __iter__ method.

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:03.668
Others, like the `Sequence` or&nbsp;
`MmutableSequence`, require a few more methods.

00:01:03.668 --> 00:01:05.835
So, if I want my type to 
be considered a sequence,

00:01:05.835 --> 00:01:10.200
I should deine a __getitem__, __len__, __contains__,
 __iter__, __reversed__, index and count function.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.496
If you want your type to be considered a&nbsp;
`Sequence`, you do need all these methods.

00:01:13.496 --> 00:01:17.830
But these are also ABCs. 
You can use them&nbsp;as base classes.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:19.663
And if you use them 
as a base class,

00:01:19.663 --> 00:01:23.280
then really you only need&nbsp;
to define things in this middle column.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.325
So, if I define `__getitem__` and `__len__`&nbsp;
and inherit from `Sequence`,

00:01:26.325 --> 00:01:31.140
then Python will provide default 
implementations&nbsp;of the things in this right column.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.081
Also, notice that there's a `Sequence` 
and&nbsp;a `MutableSequence`.

00:01:34.081 --> 00:01:38.335
There's a `Set` and a `MutableSet`, 
there's a `Mapping` and a `MutableMapping`.

00:01:38.335 --> 00:01:41.871
Remember, these are just interfaces.
So if you annotate something as being a `Mapping`,

00:01:41.871 --> 00:01:44.400
that doesn't mean it 
also can't be mutable.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.102
This distinction has more to do with
type hinting&nbsp;as a way of telling your callers

00:01:48.102 --> 00:01:52.030
that&nbsp;you're not going to modify their map, 
rather than defining a type that can't be&nbsp;mutated.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:54.840
Although that's also 
a possibility.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.136
Obviously, there are too many 
of&nbsp;these to fully cover in one video.&nbsp;

00:01:58.136 --> 00:02:01.756
So that's why I'm announcing a 15-part&nbsp;
sequence... no, I'm just kidding.

00:02:01.756 --> 00:02:04.545
There are just a few that are the 
most common&nbsp;that you'll actually use.

00:02:04.545 --> 00:02:07.080
`Iterable`: stuff you can iterate over.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.301
`Sequence`, and `MutableSequence`
 are abstract versions of a list.

00:02:11.301 --> 00:02:14.903
Then there is`Set` and `MutableSet` which 
is the abstract version of a set.

00:02:14.903 --> 00:02:18.744
`Mapping`, and `MutableMapping` 
which are the abstact version of a dictionary.

00:02:18.744 --> 00:02:22.822
I recommend just coming back to this&nbsp;table 
if you ever need to remember&nbsp;what's part of the interface.

00:02:22.822 --> 00:02:26.496
For the rest&nbsp;of this video, I'm just going 
to focus on `Mapping` and `MutableMapping`.

00:02:26.496 --> 00:02:29.670
So, on to&nbsp;our first example 
use case: type hinting.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.958
Here's a very simple function 
that's meant to&nbsp;take in a dictionary

00:02:31.958 --> 00:02:34.674
and just delete the 
entry with the smallest key.

00:02:34.674 --> 00:02:37.074
If it's empty, we 
just&nbsp;return. We don't error.

00:02:37.074 --> 00:02:39.937
We find the smallest 
key and then delete that entry.

00:02:39.937 --> 00:02:42.712
Now, I'm not&nbsp;saying that you 
should be typing all of your code all the time.

00:02:42.712 --> 00:02:46.491
I think type hinting is an&nbsp;
optional feature of Python, as it should be.

00:02:46.491 --> 00:02:49.440
One of the main benefits,&nbsp;
of course, is autocomplete.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.480
Because I haven't type hinted `d`, PyCharm is&nbsp;
giving me basically no good suggestions here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.580
But if I annotate it as a `Dict` and try again,&nbsp;
now I'm getting a lot more relevant suggestions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.604
And yes, you can get even better suggestions&nbsp;
if you specify the key type and value type.

00:03:03.604 --> 00:03:08.918
But one issue that a lot of people don't think&nbsp;about 
is how restrictive this can be for the&nbsp;caller of the function.

00:03:08.918 --> 00:03:13.621
As the author of code&nbsp;like this, it might be 
most convenient to specify an exact type here.

00:03:13.621 --> 00:03:17.343
But if this is an internal&nbsp;function and 
there may be other people calling your function,

00:03:17.343 --> 00:03:20.979
putting this type in here actually&nbsp;
becomes a restriction.

00:03:20.979 --> 00:03:22.980
Doing an `if` check, 
finding the minimum key,

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:26.760
and deleting a key&nbsp;
are not specific to the built-in `dict` type.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.600
If a user has something like a custom dictionary&nbsp;type, 
maybe they're wrapping a dictionary type,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.100
they might still want to use your function, 
and&nbsp;your implementation might still work for them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.221
But because of 
your restrictive type&nbsp;hint,

00:03:37.221 --> 00:03:38.596
now their editor 
is going to complain

00:03:38.596 --> 00:03:42.360
that they're doing something&nbsp;
wrong when, in fact, maybe it's just fine.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.166
In many cases, if you've 
hinted something to&nbsp;be a dictionary

00:03:45.166 --> 00:03:50.700
and it's a public-facing API, consider that 
a `MutableMapping`&nbsp;might be a more flexible option.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.736
Also, note that our use of 
`MutableMapping`&nbsp;helps inform our caller

00:03:53.736 --> 00:03:56.017
that we do intend to 
mutate their dictionary,

00:03:56.017 --> 00:03:59.880
which may not have been&nbsp;
apparent if we had just annotated it as a `dict`.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.487
But type hinting isn't the only 
use case for these&nbsp;abstract collections.

00:04:04.487 --> 00:04:07.348
It's possible to check, 
and a lot of code does check,

00:04:07.348 --> 00:04:12.080
whether something is&nbsp;an instance 
or a subclass of one of these ABCs at runtime.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.961
This allows you to react differently&nbsp;based 
on the structure of the object that you received.

00:04:15.961 --> 00:04:20.099
Something that is actually quite&nbsp;
useful in a dynamic language like Python.

00:04:20.099 --> 00:04:22.249
Here we're defining a `deep_min`, 
which&nbsp;is just like a `min`,

00:04:22.249 --> 00:04:25.566
but it will also traverse any 
structures like lists or dictionaries

00:04:25.566 --> 00:04:28.440
and&nbsp;go inside of them 
to find the smallest element.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.516
So even if I had this 
nested list of numbers,

00:04:30.516 --> 00:04:34.186
`deep_min` would still find this 
zero and pick that out as the minimum.

00:04:34.186 --> 00:04:39.124
If something&nbsp;is iterable, then we just recursively find 
the deep_min and then take the minimum&nbsp;of those.

00:04:39.124 --> 00:04:42.251
But specifically for mappings, 
we may not be interested in the keys.

00:04:42.251 --> 00:04:44.400
We don't want to find 
the minimum key.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.820
So if we do see a mapping, then we 
only&nbsp;recurse into the values, not the keys.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:50.911
And if we get something 
not iterable, like a number,

00:04:50.911 --> 00:04:53.492
then&nbsp;that's our base case 
and we just return the value.

00:04:53.492 --> 00:04:56.108
It's pretty common for a library 
author to&nbsp;do this kind of switching.

00:04:56.108 --> 00:05:01.393
And if they do, it would typically be based 
on one of the ABCs in&nbsp;the Collections ABC module.

00:05:01.393 --> 00:05:04.381
There's also a lot of Python 
internals that use this machinery.

00:05:04.381 --> 00:05:07.143
For&nbsp;instance, if you're using the 
`match` statement from Python 3.10,

00:05:07.143 --> 00:05:09.746
then a case like this that&nbsp;
looks like it's using a dictionary

00:05:09.746 --> 00:05:12.293
will actually match 
against any mapping type.

00:05:12.293 --> 00:05:17.003
If you want&nbsp;your custom type to pass
 these `isinstance` or `issubclass` checks,

00:05:17.003 --> 00:05:19.483
then you can, of course,&nbsp;
inherit from `Mapping`.

00:05:19.483 --> 00:05:21.992
Or if you don't want to or 
can't inherit from `Mapping`,

00:05:21.992 --> 00:05:25.860
you could also fake&nbsp;inherit 
from it by using the `register` method.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.129
This will make Python treat this class like it's&nbsp;
a subclass of `Mapping`, even if it's not.

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:32.471
Now, you're basically asking 
Python to just&nbsp;trust you on this one.

00:05:32.471 --> 00:05:35.655
So if you do this, then make sure 
that you actually implement the&nbsp;interface.

00:05:35.655 --> 00:05:37.980
Otherwise, weird things might fail.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.342
Alright, so how do we 
actually make our&nbsp;own mapping type?

00:05:40.342 --> 00:05:44.418
We're going to see by implementing an 
"Invertible Dictionary,"&nbsp;which is like a two-way dictionary.

00:05:44.418 --> 00:05:48.651
It can go from keys to values, but it 
can also&nbsp;take values and go back to keys.

00:05:48.651 --> 00:05:50.289
Of course, type hints are 
completely optional.

00:05:50.289 --> 00:05:52.293
But I think&nbsp;it actually clears 
things up in this case.

00:05:52.293 --> 00:05:54.457
So I will go ahead and 
put them in.

00:05:54.457 --> 00:05:57.281
`KT`&nbsp;and `VT` stand for key 
type and value type.

00:05:57.281 --> 00:06:00.520
We're going to implement this `InvertibleDict`&nbsp;
by just having two dictionaries

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:03.232
A forward dictionary and 
a backward dictionary.

00:06:03.232 --> 00:06:06.768
Forward&nbsp;maps keys to values, 
and backward maps values to keys.

00:06:06.768 --> 00:06:10.234
We'll go ahead and use slots because&nbsp;
why not? See my video on slots.

00:06:10.234 --> 00:06:13.069
For this example, we want to 
actually inherit from "MutableMapping"

00:06:13.069 --> 00:06:17.332
because we want to take advantage 
of the mix in default implementation methods.

00:06:17.332 --> 00:06:18.857
Going back to this one 
with the table,

00:06:18.857 --> 00:06:23.478
we see that if we define __getitem__, 
__setitem__, __delitem__, __iter__ and __len__,

00:06:23.478 --> 00:06:26.664
then Python will define all 
the rest of these methods for us.

00:06:26.664 --> 00:06:29.519
Meaning we don't have to define 
any of these standard dictionary functions,

00:06:29.519 --> 00:06:32.111
or any of these mutating 
dictionary functions.

00:06:32.111 --> 00:06:37.336
The abstract base class will provide default 
implementations just using the files that we did provide.

00:06:37.336 --> 00:06:42.394
So we go back to our implementation
 and these are the five we actually need to define.

00:06:42.394 --> 00:06:47.233
After we define those five, then we could use 
our class just like we use dictionary in most cases.

00:06:47.233 --> 00:06:51.470
Everything else is either extra 
functionality like inverse function

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:54.399
or typical customization 
point like a __repr__

00:06:54.399 --> 00:06:58.112
or custom implementation of something 
that could have been provided by the ABC.

00:06:58.112 --> 00:06:59.939
We will see 
more on that later.

00:06:59.939 --> 00:07:04.367
Of course, the way we write these 
five depends on the structure of our class. 

00:07:04.367 --> 00:07:07.169
The way I've designed things, our class 
just stores the forward and backward dictionaries

00:07:07.169 --> 00:07:10.080
which we initialized in 
the init function.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.655
Typically, we would just have the 
user pass&nbsp;in the forward dictionary,

00:07:12.655 --> 00:07:15.054
and then we would just 
compute the backward one.

00:07:15.054 --> 00:07:19.183
Depending on&nbsp;what they provide, we
 either start with an empty forward and backward

00:07:19.183 --> 00:07:22.320
or we start with the&nbsp;forward they 
give us and compute the backward.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.771
Or, if they provide this backward parameter&nbsp;,
it's kind of like a secret parameter,&nbsp;then we just use it.

00:07:26.771 --> 00:07:29.488
It's kind of a convention in&nbsp;Python 
that if you have a keyword-only argument

00:07:29.488 --> 00:07:33.381
that starts with an underscore, it's 
kind of&nbsp;like a private implementation detail.

00:07:33.381 --> 00:07:35.143
Of course, it doesn't actually 
stop anyone from using&nbsp;it,

00:07:35.143 --> 00:07:37.109
but that's the way that 
we're going to be using it.

00:07:37.109 --> 00:07:41.406
So our own internal functions&nbsp;like the `inverse` function 
might want to provide this backward argument,

00:07:41.406 --> 00:07:44.236
but a&nbsp;typical user 
shouldn't be providing it.

00:07:44.236 --> 00:07:46.549
The specific details of __init__ 
are&nbsp;actually not that important.

00:07:46.549 --> 00:07:50.212
Just keep in mind that `forward` and 
`backward`&nbsp;are just plain old dictionaries.

00:07:50.212 --> 00:07:53.705
So when someone goes to look 
up&nbsp;a key, how do they do it?

00:07:53.705 --> 00:07:57.999
Well, looking up a key is pretty simple. 
We just look&nbsp;it up using the forward dictionary.

00:07:57.999 --> 00:08:02.346
Similarly, the length of our dictionary 
is just the length&nbsp;of the forward dictionary.

00:08:02.346 --> 00:08:04.689
And the way that we iterate 
over the keys of our dictionary

00:08:04.689 --> 00:08:07.860
is just&nbsp;iterating over the 
keys of the forward dictionary.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:13.208
The mutating functions like __setitem__&nbsp;and __delitem__ 
do have to involve the&nbsp;backward dictionary, though.

00:08:13.208 --> 00:08:18.082
We'll go through it slowly, but just keep in mind 
that the mathematical&nbsp;invariant of this class is that

00:08:18.082 --> 00:08:22.827
the forward and backward dictionaries
 represent inverse&nbsp;mappings of each other.

00:08:22.827 --> 00:08:25.977
They should define a mathematical
 one-to-one correspondence.

00:08:25.977 --> 00:08:29.121
A key maps&nbsp;to a value from 
the forward direction

00:08:29.121 --> 00:08:33.825
if and only if the value maps 
back to the key in the backward&nbsp;direction.

00:08:33.825 --> 00:08:36.360
So let's take a 
look at __setitem__.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:40.274
Taking a look at the end here, what 
we ultimately&nbsp;do is maintain that class invariant

00:08:40.274 --> 00:08:43.605
that the forward and backward 
are inverse maps of each&nbsp;other.

00:08:43.605 --> 00:08:48.945
In the forward map, we map key to value, and in 
the backward map, we map value to key&nbsp;- simple as that.

00:08:48.945 --> 00:08:55.071
All the rest of this is to handle the cases where 
either the key or the&nbsp;value already existed in the dictionary.

00:08:55.071 --> 00:08:59.220
This checks whether the value that we're trying&nbsp;
to set already has a key associated with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:03.120
If it does, and it's a different key, then&nbsp;
we're trying to break the class invariant.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.500
We're trying to create two different keys&nbsp;
that map to the same value - that's an error.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.984
This would happen if I started with the dictionary&nbsp;
like `{1: 'A'}` and then tried to set `{2:&nbsp;&nbsp;'A'}`.

00:09:11.984 --> 00:09:13.890
That's perfectly fine 
in a normal&nbsp;dictionary.

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:16.415
But this is supposed to be 
an invertible dictionary.

00:09:16.415 --> 00:09:19.473
So if I had `1`&nbsp;mapping to 
`'A'` and `2` mapping to `'A'`,

00:09:19.473 --> 00:09:21.572
then what would 
the inverse of `'A'` be?

00:09:21.572 --> 00:09:24.074
That&nbsp;would be an error, 
so we just raise an exception.

00:09:24.074 --> 00:09:29.132
This next case is for if the key that we're&nbsp;
trying to set already existed in the dictionary.

00:09:29.132 --> 00:09:31.766
For instance, say 
that we had 1 mapping to A,

00:09:31.766 --> 00:09:34.944
so in&nbsp;the backward dictionary, 
we have A mapping to 1.

00:09:34.944 --> 00:09:38.740
We could set 1 mapping to B 
and B mapping&nbsp;to 1. That's fine.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:41.627
But that backward dictionary 
would still have A mapping to 1&nbsp;in it.

00:09:41.627 --> 00:09:43.560
So we need 
to delete it first.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:48.540
It's okay whether or not the values were
 equal. We're just going to reset it right here anyway.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:52.437
Then we get to __delitem__, which
is&nbsp;thankfully much simpler.

00:09:52.437 --> 00:09:56.489
We're given the key, we look up the value, 
and then delete it from&nbsp;both directions.

00:09:56.489 --> 00:09:59.675
Not too much going on there. 
And that's all five!

00:09:59.675 --> 00:10:01.465
Because&nbsp;we defined these 
five, we can now use

00:10:01.465 --> 00:10:05.640
our `InvertibleDict` just&nbsp;like 
we would any other mutable mapping.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:10.800
Now, the most fun/elegant part, in my&nbsp;
opinion - how do we access inverse elements?

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.156
You might be tempted to do 
something like make an&nbsp;inverse_getitem function

00:10:14.156 --> 00:10:17.774
that takes values and gives 
you back keys, and you could do that.

00:10:17.774 --> 00:10:20.115
But&nbsp;you would end 
up rewriting a lot of code.

00:10:20.115 --> 00:10:25.560
You need the inverse __getitem__, __setitem__,&nbsp;
__delitem__, __iter__, and __len__.__get__

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.868
But wait, those are all functions that are&nbsp;
provided by `Mapping`.

00:10:28.868 --> 00:10:31.549
I'm just describing another mapping.

00:10:31.549 --> 00:10:36.420
So what we do instead is&nbsp;provide one extra 
function to access the inverse.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:41.677
We make a `.inv` property that just 
swaps the&nbsp;forward and backward maps.

00:10:41.677 --> 00:10:45.775
Forward and backward are already inverses of 
each other, so I don't&nbsp;need to do any extra work.

00:10:45.775 --> 00:10:49.703
I just create a new `InvertibleDict` whose 
forward map is my backward&nbsp;map

00:10:49.703 --> 00:10:52.500
and whose backward 
map is my forward map.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.197
Then, check it out - 
we can use it just&nbsp;like this.

00:10:55.197 --> 00:10:57.420
We create an `InvertibleDict`.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:00.183
We can print it or print its inverse.

00:11:00.183 --> 00:11:02.452
We&nbsp;can check that 1 maps to A.

00:11:02.452 --> 00:11:06.223
And we can check that 
the inverse of A maps to 1.

00:11:06.223 --> 00:11:09.060
Then we can assign the 
inverse of D as 4.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.521
And then check that 4 maps to D.

00:11:11.521 --> 00:11:16.311
This&nbsp;works because the function and its inverse 
are sharing the same underlying dictionaries.

00:11:16.311 --> 00:11:22.224
Modifying the function is modifying the inverse, 
and modifying the inverse is modifying&nbsp;the function.

00:11:22.224 --> 00:11:25.356
Because I defined a repr, 
we can print these things out.

00:11:25.356 --> 00:11:28.088
the repr only shows 
you the forward dictionary.

00:11:28.088 --> 00:11:31.716
So if we print the function, we see 
`{1: 'A',&nbsp;2: 'B', 3: 'C'}`.

00:11:31.716 --> 00:11:36.217
And if we print the inverse, we see 
`{'A': 1, 'B': 2, 'C': 3}`.

00:11:36.217 --> 00:11:40.315
And then the&nbsp;second lines, 
we can see that setting the inverse `'D'` as `4`

00:11:40.315 --> 00:11:42.220
actually sets both of them.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:45.621
This&nbsp;can be extremely useful when you're 
translating between two different representations

00:11:45.621 --> 00:11:47.888
of a fixed&nbsp;number of objects.

00:11:47.888 --> 00:11:52.822
A way to tell if you might benefit from using 
this kind of invertible&nbsp;dictionary is to ask yourself:

00:11:52.822 --> 00:11:58.248
Do I have just two dictionaries that have 
the same data mapping&nbsp;in opposite directions?

00:11:58.248 --> 00:12:02.105
Like, if you had two dictionaries 
`int` to `char` and `char` to&nbsp;`int`,

00:12:02.105 --> 00:12:06.906
That's the kind of situation where you might have 
data on either side and want to convert&nbsp;between them.

00:12:06.906 --> 00:12:09.000
So feel free to use this idea!

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.084
Alright, I do have one more&nbsp;
practical piece of advice on the&nbsp;matter.

00:12:12.084 --> 00:12:15.237
We saw an implementation that&nbsp;
was inheriting from `MutableMapping`.

00:12:15.237 --> 00:12:18.676
And `MutableMapping` was 
providing a bunch of&nbsp;functionality.

00:12:18.676 --> 00:12:24.695
It defines `pop`, `popitem`, `clear`, `update`, 
all these things, and&nbsp;it defines them correctly.

00:12:24.695 --> 00:12:28.297
But that doesn't mean that these 
default implementations&nbsp;are the most efficient.

00:12:28.297 --> 00:12:30.801
For instance, look 
at how it's doing `clear`.

00:12:30.801 --> 00:12:33.219
It's&nbsp;basically got a 
`while True` and tries to pop items.

00:12:33.219 --> 00:12:36.900
It's popping items off one&nbsp;
at a time until it gets a `KeyError`.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:41.160
Now, for a general `MutableMapping`, that is&nbsp;
sort of the most general thing that you can do,&nbsp;

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.980
that would be correct - pop&nbsp;
things off one at a time.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:48.277
But our implementation is using two dictionaries,&nbsp;
which both themselves have `clear` methods.

00:12:48.277 --> 00:12:50.912
We don't need to pop 
things off one at a time.

00:12:50.912 --> 00:12:54.907
So for efficiency sake, you may find
 yourself re-implementing a lot of those functions

00:12:54.907 --> 00:12:57.996
that&nbsp;you were supposed to 
get for free as mix-ins.

00:12:57.996 --> 00:13:00.264
From my own experience, I've definitely 
found&nbsp;that to be the case,

00:13:00.264 --> 00:13:04.295
especially for the more 
complex interfaces like `MutableMapping`.

00:13:04.295 --> 00:13:08.795
And after a few months or maybe a year, 
I've re-implemented everything that was a mix-in.

00:13:08.795 --> 00:13:11.640
At which point, I may even 
no longer inherit from `MutableMapping`

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:16.140
and instead just use a typing&nbsp;
`Generic`, or not inherit from anything at all.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.598
Just remember 
that if you do this,

00:13:17.598 --> 00:13:21.000
you will&nbsp;probably want to make 
your class register with `MutableMapping`

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.960
so that it will still be&nbsp;treated as 
a `MutableMapping`, a mapping type.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.960
And finally, for the more advanced&nbsp;
typing users, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:32.286
I'm not going to talk about one of the bigger&nbsp;
benefits of using these interfaces.

00:13:32.286 --> 00:13:35.205
It has to do with covariance 
and contravariance&nbsp;of type variables,

00:13:35.205 --> 00:13:39.196
which, unfortunately, I just have 
not set up the prerequisites to&nbsp;talk about.

00:13:39.196 --> 00:13:41.952
But if your extra studios go ahead 
and look those up in the typing library.

00:13:41.952 --> 00:13:44.520
I'm sure there's plenty 
for you to learn.

